---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { AUTHOR, SITE_URL, SITE_TITLE, SITE_DESCRIPTION } from "../consts";

const links = AUTHOR.links.filter((l) => l.href);

// Periodical describes the publication. Person, linked as its author, is what
// ties the accounts together for a search engine without making the site
// itself read as a personal page.
const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Periodical",
  name: SITE_TITLE,
  url: SITE_URL,
  description: SITE_DESCRIPTION,
  author: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: AUTHOR.name,
    sameAs: links.map((l) => l.href),
  },
};
---

<Base title="About" description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}>
  <div class="page">
    <div class="shell">
      <span class="kicker">About</span>
      <h1>What this is</h1>

      <div class="prose column">
        <p>
          Teximos is a publication about technology and the people shipping
          it. Model releases, product decisions, the policy starting to shape
          the field, the science underneath it and the occasional real
          incident with the code still attached. What just changed, what it
          actually does and what it costs when it breaks.
        </p>
        <p>
          The test for a piece is not whether it is about AI or about any
          particular company. It is whether it explains one specific thing
          that is true, written so that anyone who works near software can
          follow it without being an engineer. No tutorials. No roundups. No
          opinion with the reasoning left out.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some of it is reported from the outside, off the primary sources.
          Some of it is written from the inside, out of systems I built and
          broke myself, which is the only reason the numbers and the offending
          code can be shown in full rather than paraphrased.
        </p>

        <h2>Who runs it</h2>
        <p>
          {AUTHOR.name}. Teximos is independent and has no sponsor and no
          product to sell you. It exists because the interesting parts of this
          industry are usually the specific ones, and those are the parts that
          go unwritten.
        </p>
        <p>
          Corrections are welcome. If something here is wrong, it is worth
          fixing in place rather than leaving.
        </p>
      </div>

      {
        links.length > 0 && (
          <ul class="profiles">
            {links.map((l) => (
              <li>
                <span class="k">{l.label}</span>
                <a href={l.href}>{l.href.replace(/^https?:\/\//, "")}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    is:inline
    set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}
  />
</Base>
